**Read:** [Cleaning and surface properties — Mauro Taborelli](../sources/Cleaning-and-Surface-Properties-Taborelli.lingua#r_1RDARBEFXDAW9Y2DNTJ2QBEP2E), rinsing and complex shapes; [Cleaning technology in semiconductor device manufacturing VIII — ECS proceedings](../sources/Cleaning-Technology-in-Semiconductor-Device-Manufacturing-VIII.lingua#r_1CVF9XCJR7E3J37X5150BDS5Y2), Chang and colleagues on printed pages 27–33.

Rinsing replaces or removes process liquid and what it carries. Incoming water quality, contact materials, retained films, pockets, adsorption and temperature define the starting problem. A nominal volume passed through equipment is not the volume that exchanged with every surface.

The semiconductor rinsing study compares remaining defects with material-loss and surface observations. Use those distinct outcomes to examine a transition from cleaning agent to rinse liquid. A change that improves one count can also change the substrate.

**Work:** Draw liquid locations on the chosen part: exposed film, blind hole, joint, porous region and flow path. Identify the incoming and outgoing compositions and the point-of-use measurement. Develop an ideal balance first, then mark each place where incomplete mixing, reaction or retained material invalidates it.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
